extends layout

block head
    link(rel='stylesheet' href='/css/manual.css')

block content
    h1 Ausschnitt Manual

    p.lead.
        Ausschnitt is a personal webcomic reader. Point it at a webcomic's own
        site once, and it crawls new pages for you, automatically crops each
        page into individual panels, and shows them sized for your screen -
        as one continuous scroll or one panel at a time - instead of a whole
        strip zoomed out on a small screen.

    .manual-toc.mb-4
        strong On this page:
        ul
            li: a(href='#adding') Adding a comic
            li: a(href='#reading') Reading
            li: a(href='#icons') Icons
            li: a(href='#bookmarks') Your reading position, bookmarks &amp; ignoring pages
            li: a(href='#splitting') How automatic panel splitting works
            li: a(href='#settings') Settings
            li: a(href='#managing') Managing comics
            li: a(href='#install') Installing it as an app
            li: a(href='#notifications') New-page notifications

    h2#adding Adding a comic
    p.
        From the home page, click #[strong + Add Comic] and give it the
        comic's URL and a name. You'll land on the #[strong Select Elements]
        screen next - the one-time setup step that teaches Ausschnitt how to
        find things on that comic's own pages. (If another account on this
        server already added the same comic, you skip this step and go
        straight to the reader.)
    p.
        The comic's page loads in a preview below. A label above it tells
        you what to click next - in order: the #[strong comic image], then
        the #[strong Next], #[strong Back], #[strong First], and
        #[strong Latest] page links/buttons.
    p.
        Each click is recorded as a small "fingerprint" (tag, class, position
        in the page) rather than a fixed link, so navigation keeps working
        even after the comic's site gets a minor redesign.
    p.
        #[strong Next] and #[strong Latest] are often disabled on the
        comic's own newest page (there's nothing to go to yet). If clicking
        one says it doesn't lead anywhere, tap #[strong Browse Site], click
        through to an older page, and select it from there - your progress
        so far is kept.
    p.
        Once all five are selected, the comic is saved automatically and you
        land straight in the reader.

    h2#reading Reading
    p.
        Open a comic from the home page to continue at your saved reading
        position (or the very first panel, the first time). New pages are
        fetched and split into panels automatically the first time you reach
        them, so moving past a page Ausschnitt hasn't seen yet takes a moment
        longer than usual.
    p.
        There are two ways to read. The default is set in #[strong Settings]
        (⚙ in the top bar, #[strong Default reading mode]); any comic can override it from its
        #[strong Settings].
    h3.h5 Endless scroll (default)
    ul
        li Panels are shown one under the other - just scroll. More load automatically as you near either end.
        li Your position is saved as you scroll, and again when you leave the page or switch apps.
        li The bottom bar has #[strong First] ⏮ and #[strong Latest] ⏭. It can be hidden for more room - for every comic from #[strong Settings] (#[strong Endless scroll bottom bar]), or per comic from its #[strong Settings].
    h3.h5 Panel-by-panel
    ul
        li One panel at a time, sized to fill the screen. Pinch to zoom in.
        li #[strong Swipe] left/right on the image, or use the bottom bar: First ⏮ / Back ◀ / first panel of this page ⇤ / Next ▶ / Latest ⏭.
        li Your position is saved with every Next/Back.

    h2#icons Icons
    p.
        The same icon always means the same thing, whether it's in the
        reader's top bar or the bar at the top of every other page:
    ul
        li 🏠 #[strong Home] - back to your comic list.
        li.
            🔖 #[strong Bookmarks] - in the reader, save the panel you're on
            under a name, or jump to one you saved earlier for this comic;
            elsewhere, the full bookmarks manager.
        li.
            🕘 #[strong Resume history] - reader only; this comic's recent
            "Save Resume Point" checkpoints.
        li.
            🚫 #[strong Ignore] - reader only; hides the page you're looking at
            from now on. Tap once, then tap #[strong OK?] within 3
            seconds. A popup with #[strong Undo] appears right after, in case
            it was the wrong page.
        li.
            ⚙ #[strong Settings] - in the reader, this comic's reading mode,
            resume history, and ignored pages; elsewhere, your general
            settings (theme, reading defaults, notifications, disk space).
        li 👥 #[strong Admin] - manage user accounts. Only with multiple accounts, only for admins, and never in the reader.
        li 🚪 #[strong Log out] - only with multiple accounts, and never in the reader.
        li ❓ #[strong Manual] - this page.

    h2#bookmarks Your reading position, bookmarks &amp; ignoring pages
    p.
        Ausschnitt keeps one saved reading position per comic - where you
        continue next time. Reading normally moves it along with you; there's
        nothing to click.
    p.
        Jumping somewhere else - #[strong First], #[strong Latest], a
        bookmark, or an old resume checkpoint - does #[em not] move your
        saved position; it's just a look around. While you're away from it,
        a banner says #[em "Jumped away from your last spot"] with two
        buttons: #[strong Resume] takes you back, and
        #[strong Save Resume Point] makes where you are now your new saved
        position. Reading your way back onto your saved position hides the
        banner too, and saving carries on as normal from there.
    p.
        Each #[strong Save Resume Point] also adds a checkpoint to this
        comic's #[strong resume history] (the 🕘 panel), which keeps the 10
        most recent. The position being replaced is added as well, so a
        save never loses where you were before. Tapping a checkpoint only
        takes a look, like a jump; the full list, with delete, is on the
        #[strong History] tab of the comic's #[strong Settings] page.
    p.
        #[strong Bookmarks] are separate from all of that: named, permanent
        spots you create yourself, as many as you like. Open the 🔖 panel in
        the reader to save the panel you're on (it suggests a name, which you
        can change) or jump to one of this comic's bookmarks. To rename or
        delete bookmarks across every comic, use the
        #[a(href='/bookmarks') Bookmarks] page.
    p.
        #[strong Ignore] a page you never want to see again (a filler page,
        an arc you're skipping): it disappears from the scroll feed, and
        Next/Back step over it. Ignored the wrong one? Tap #[strong Undo] on
        the popup right after - the page comes back and you're returned to
        it. Ignored pages can also be restored any time from the
        #[strong Ignored] tab of the comic's #[strong Settings] page.

    h2#splitting How automatic panel splitting works
    p.
        After a page's image is downloaded, Ausschnitt looks for a real panel
        grid on it and, if it's confident, crops each panel out into its own
        image - that's the "Ausschnitt" (German for "cutout") the app is
        named after. It isn't guessing panel positions from a template; it
        actually examines the downloaded pixels every time, so it copes with
        different comics (and different eras of the #[em same] comic) having
        completely different layouts.
    p.
        The examples below use plain numbered boxes instead of a real comic,
        run through the exact same detector the app uses on real pages.

    //- Original on the left, the cut-out panels on the right - stacked in
    //- reading order, each at the (half as wide) column's full width, the
    //- way the reader shows them.
    mixin splitExample(name, pageAlt, panels, label)
        .split-example
            figure.split-page
                img(src=`/img/manual/${name}-page.png` alt=pageAlt)
                figcaption Downloaded page
            .split-arrow(aria-hidden='true')
                svg(viewBox='0 0 40 24' width='40' height='24')
                    line(x1='2' y1='12' x2='34' y2='12')
                    polyline(points='27,5 35,12 27,19')
                span= label
            .split-panels
                each alt, i in panels
                    figure
                        img(src=`/img/manual/${name}-panel${i + 1}.png` alt=alt)

    h3 A clean grid
    p.text-muted.small Four evenly-spaced panels with plain white gutters between them - the common case.
    +splitExample('grid2x2', 'A page with a 2x2 grid of numbered panels', ['Panel 1', 'Panel 2', 'Panel 3', 'Panel 4'], '4 panels')

    h3 An irregular grid
    p.text-muted.small Panels don't have to be the same size or line up into a neat table - two panels on top, one wide panel underneath.
    +splitExample('irregular', 'A page with two panels on top and one wide panel below', ['Panel 1', 'Panel 2', 'Panel 3'], '3 panels')

    h3 When it's not confident: left whole
    p.text-muted.small.
        A divider only counts if it's the same color as the page's own
        background - a plain gutter. A solid line drawn directly between two
        panels with no background gap around it (common in hand-inked
        comics) is a different color, so it isn't trusted as a divider even
        though it's obviously two panels to a human eye. More generally,
        whenever a page doesn't look like a clear, confident grid, Ausschnitt
        leaves it as a single panel rather than guessing - an unsplit page is
        a minor inconvenience; a wrong split (cutting through a face or a
        speech bubble) is a much worse one, so the detector always fails
        toward "do nothing" instead of "guess."
    +splitExample('fallback', 'Two panels separated only by a drawn black line with no background gap around it', ['Left unsplit as one panel'], 'left whole')

    h2#settings Settings
    p.
        There are two kinds of settings: your general #[a(href='/settings') Settings],
        which apply everywhere, and each comic's own settings, which can
        override some of them for just that comic. Both save as soon as you
        change something - there's no Save button.
    h3.h5 General settings
    p.
        Open them with ⚙ in the top bar of any page except the reader (in the
        reader, ⚙ opens that comic's settings instead).
    ul
        li #[strong Theme] - System (follows your device), Light or Dark. Remembered per device, so your phone and computer can differ.
        li #[strong Default reading mode] - Endless Scroll or Paged (panel-by-panel), for every comic that doesn't choose its own.
        li #[strong Endless scroll bottom bar] - show or hide the First ⏮ / Latest ⏭ bar in endless scroll.
        li #[strong 🔔 New-page notifications], #[strong Check for new pages] and #[strong Tapping a notification opens] - see #[a(href='#notifications') New-page notifications].
        li #[strong Storage] - how much space your comics use and how much is left on the server, plus #[strong Clear all] (single account only, see #[a(href='#managing') Managing comics]).
    h3.h5 A comic's settings
    p.
        Open them with ⚙ in the reader, or the comic's #[strong Settings]
        button on the home page. Three tabs:
    ul
        li.
            #[strong General] - reading mode, endless scroll bottom bar,
            whether going #[strong Back] onto the previous page (panel-by-panel)
            starts at its first panel or its last, whether to be notified about
            this comic's new pages, and where tapping its notification takes
            you. #[strong Default] follows your general settings - the hint
            under each setting shows what that currently is.
        li #[strong History] - this comic's resume checkpoints; delete any you don't need.
        li #[strong Ignored] - every page you've ignored, sortable by URL or date (↑/↓); #[strong Undo] brings one back.

    h2#managing Managing comics
    p On the home page:
    ul
        li #[strong Delete] - removes the comic from your list. If other accounts on this server also read it, it stays available for them; its downloaded pages are only removed once nobody has it any more.
        li #[strong Clear] - deletes a comic's downloaded pages/panels to free space or force a fresh download, but keeps the comic and its Select Elements setup (#[strong Clear all] in #[strong Settings] does this for every comic). Only available with a single account, since other accounts may be reading the same downloads.
        li each comic's disk usage - #[strong Settings] shows the total and the server's free disk space.

    h2#install Installing it as an app
    p.
        Ausschnitt can live on your phone's home screen like an app - full
        screen, no browser bars, its own icon. No app store is involved, and
        it updates itself whenever the server does.
    ul
        li #[strong iPhone/iPad (Safari)]: tap the Share button, then #[strong Add to Home Screen].
        li #[strong Android (Chrome)]: open the ⋮ menu, then #[strong Install app] (or #[strong Add to Home screen]).
    p.
        The app still needs your Ausschnitt server - it's the same site, just
        without the browser around it. If the server can't be reached, you'll
        see a short notice with a #[strong Try again] button instead of a
        browser error. The installed app keeps its own login, separate from
        your browser's, so you may need to log in once inside it.

    h2#notifications New-page notifications
    p.
        Ausschnitt can check your comics for new pages in the background and
        send a notification to your phone or computer when one appears.
    ul
        li Turn them on per device in #[strong Settings] (#[strong 🔔 New-page notifications] → #[strong Turn on]), then use #[strong Send test] to make sure they arrive.
        li #[strong Check for new pages] in #[strong Settings] sets how often each comic's site is asked - or #[strong never]. Comics are only checked while at least one device has notifications on.
        li Mute a single comic with #[strong Notify me about new pages] in that comic's #[strong Settings].
        li #[strong Tapping a notification opens] chooses where it takes you: your #[strong resume point], or #[strong the newest page] - a jump like ⏭, so your resume point stays where it was until you save a new one. Set your default in #[strong Settings]; any comic can override it in its #[strong Settings].
    p.
        Notifications need the server to be reached over #[strong HTTPS]. On an
        iPhone or iPad, add Ausschnitt to your Home Screen first (see above)
        and turn them on from the installed app - Safari itself doesn't
        offer them.
